---
layout: default
progress: true
---
<%
  release = latest_release
  post = release && release_post(release)
  runs = site.data.runs
  grid = site.data.departments.select { |department| department[:plugin] == "rubocop" }.sort_by { |department| -department[:cops].size }
  counts = grid.flat_map { |department| department[:cops] }.map { |cop| cop[:status] }.tally
%>
<section class="hero" data-sec>
  <div>
    <h1>
      <img class="logo-light" src="<%= relative_url "/images/rubo-logo-horizontal.png" %>" alt="RuboCop" width="720" height="230">
      <img class="logo-dark" src="<%= relative_url "/images/rubo-logo-horizontal-dark.png" %>" alt="RuboCop" width="720" height="230">
    </h1>
    <p class="comment"># <%= site.metadata.tagline %></p>
    <p class="lede">RuboCop checks your Ruby code against the community <a href="<%= site.metadata.style_guide_url %>">Ruby Style Guide</a>, points out what's off and fixes most of it for you. It knows about bugs, style, layout and complexity, it bends to your team's taste, and it's been at it since 2012.</p>

    <div class="install">
      <div class="install-tabs" role="tablist">
        <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="true" data-cmd="gem install rubocop" data-prompt="$" data-note="">gem</button>
        <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-cmd="gem 'rubocop', require: false" data-prompt="#" data-note="In your Gemfile. RuboCop is a standalone tool, so nothing needs to require it.">bundler</button>
      </div>
      <div class="install-line"><span class="p">$</span><code>gem install rubocop</code><button type="button" class="copy link-button">copy</button></div>
    </div>
    <p class="install-note" id="install-note" hidden></p>

    <p class="meta">
      <% if release %>
        Latest release: <a href="<%= release[:url] %>"><%= release[:version] %></a> (<%= release[:date].strftime("%-d %b %Y") %>)<% if post %>, and <a href="<%= post[:url] %>">the write-up</a><% end %>.
      <% else %>
        Latest release: <a href="<%= site.metadata.github_url %>/releases"><%= site.data.rubocop.version %></a>.
      <% end %>
      <br>
      New here? <a href="<%= site.metadata.docs_url %>usage/getting_started.html">Getting started</a> takes five minutes.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="term" aria-label="RuboCop runs on a small project">
    <%= terminal_tabs "run", "rubocop" => runs[:lint], "rubocop --diff" => runs[:diff], "rubocop -a" => runs[:fix] %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="s" id="cops" data-sec>
  <div class="txt">
    <h2>What it checks</h2>
    <p>Every check is a <em>cop</em>, and cops live in departments. <code>Lint</code> catches code that's probably a bug, <code>Style</code>, <code>Layout</code> and <code>Naming</code> keep a codebase consistent, <code>Metrics</code> flags methods and classes that got too big, and <code>Security</code> spots the usual footguns.</p>
    <p>Most cops come straight from a guideline in the style guide, and most of them can fix what they find.</p>
    <p class="more"><a href="<%= relative_url "/cops/" %>">Browse all the cops &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="cops-grid" data-src="<%= relative_url "/cops.json" %>">
    <div class="cops-head">Inspecting <%= counts.values.sum %> cops</div>
    <% grid.each do |department| %>
      <div class="dept" data-name="<%= department[:name] %>" data-fixable="<%= department[:cops].count { |cop| cop[:autocorrect] } %>">
        <span class="dept-name"><a href="<%= department[:url] %>"><%= department[:name] %>/</a></span>
        <span class="dept-n"><%= department[:cops].size %></span>
        <span class="cells"><% department[:cops].each do |cop| %><a href="<%= cop[:url] %>" class="<%= cop[:status] %>" aria-label="<%= cop[:name] %>"></a><% end %></span>
      </div>
    <% end %>
    <div class="legend"><span class="sq">on by default</span><span class="sq pending">pending (on in the next major release)</span><span class="sq disabled">opt-in</span></div>
    <div class="readout" aria-live="polite">Each square is a cop. <%= counts["enabled"] %> are on by default, <%= counts["pending"] %> are pending until the next major release and <%= counts["disabled"] %> are opt-in. Hover one to see what it does.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="s" data-sec>
  <div class="txt">
    <h2>What it fixes</h2>
    <p><code>rubocop -a</code> applies the corrections that can't change what your code does. <code>rubocop -A</code> goes further and applies the ones that might, so run it with your tests nearby.</p>
    <p>Want to look before you leap? <code>--diff</code> prints the patch and leaves your files alone. The <code>Layout</code> cops make RuboCop a formatter too, and <code>rubocop -x</code> runs just those.</p>
    <p class="more">
      <a href="<%= site.metadata.docs_url %>usage/autocorrect.html">How autocorrection works &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="term">
    <%= terminal runs[:fix_all] %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="s" data-sec>
  <div class="txt">
    <h2>Make it yours</h2>
    <p>The defaults follow the style guide, but Ruby has never had one true style and RuboCop doesn't pretend otherwise. Most cops support several styles, and it's all set in a single <code>.rubocop.yml</code>, which can inherit from a shared one.</p>
    <p>Adopting it on an old codebase? <code>rubocop --auto-gen-config</code> records every existing offense in <code>.rubocop_todo.yml</code>, so new code meets the standard today and the old debt gets paid off one cop at a time.</p>
    <p>For real-world examples, see how <a href="https://github.com/rails/rails/blob/main/.rubocop.yml">Rails</a>, <a href="https://github.com/gitlabhq/gitlabhq/blob/master/.rubocop.yml">GitLab</a>, <a href="https://github.com/discourse/discourse/blob/main/.rubocop.yml">Discourse</a> and <a href="https://github.com/mastodon/mastodon/blob/main/.rubocop.yml">Mastodon</a> set it up.</p>
    <p class="more"><a href="<%= site.metadata.docs_url %>configuration.html">Configuration &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="yaml">
<pre><span class="y-c"># .rubocop.yml</span>
<span class="y-k">inherit_from</span>: <span class="y-s">.rubocop_todo.yml</span>

<span class="y-k">AllCops</span>:
  <span class="y-k">TargetRubyVersion</span>: <span class="y-v">3.4</span>
  <span class="y-k">NewCops</span>: <span class="y-v">enable</span>

<span class="y-k">Style/StringLiterals</span>:
  <span class="y-k">EnforcedStyle</span>: <span class="y-v">double_quotes</span>   <span class="y-c"># we know, we know</span>

<span class="y-k">Metrics/MethodLength</span>:
  <span class="y-k">Max</span>: <span class="y-v">15</span>
  <span class="y-k">Exclude</span>:
    - <span class="y-s">db/migrate/**/*</span></pre>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="s" data-sec>
  <div class="txt">
    <h2>Plugins</h2>
    <p>The core knows Ruby. Plugins teach it about the frameworks and libraries around it. The RuboCop team maintains the ones on the right, and there are plenty more out there, from <code>rubocop-graphql</code> to <code>rubocop-sorbet</code>.</p>
    <p>Writing your own cops is easier than it sounds, and a project can keep a few in-house cops for its own conventions.</p>
    <p class="more"><a href="<%= site.metadata.docs_url %>plugins.html">All the plugins &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="yaml">
    <% width = site.data.plugins.map { |plugin| plugin[:name].size }.max %>
<pre><span class="y-k">plugins</span>:
<% site.data.plugins.each do |plugin| %>  - <a class="y-s" href="https://github.com/rubocop/<%= plugin[:name] %>"><%= plugin[:name] %></a><%= " " * (width - plugin[:name].size) %>  <span class="y-c"># <%= plugin[:note] %></span>
<% end %></pre>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="s" data-sec>
  <div class="txt">
    <h2>Where it runs</h2>
    <p>On your machine, in your editor, in CI and, these days, inside your coding agent. The output fits wherever it ends up.</p>
    <dl class="flags">
      <dt>--lsp</dt><dd>A built-in language server: offenses as you type and format-on-save in VS Code, RubyMine, Emacs, Neovim, Helix, Zed and friends.</dd>
      <dt>--format github</dt><dd>Offenses show up as annotations on pull requests. <code>sarif</code> feeds code scanning, <code>junit</code> the rest of CI.</dd>
      <dt>--changed</dt><dd>Only looks at the files git says changed.</dd>
      <dt>--mcp</dt><dd>Inspection and autocorrection for coding agents, over the Model Context Protocol.</dd>
      <dt>--server</dt><dd>Keeps a warm process around, so runs start instantly.</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>
  <div class="term">
    <%= terminal_tabs "format", "-f offenses" => runs[:offenses], "-f worst" => runs[:worst], "-f github" => runs[:github], "-f json" => runs[:json] %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="s" id="news" data-sec>
  <div class="txt">
    <h2>News</h2>
    <p>A new release lands every few weeks. The <a href="<%= site.metadata.changelog_url %>">changelog</a> has every detail, and the bigger ones get a write-up on <a href="https://metaredux.com/tags/#rubocop">Meta Redux</a>.</p>
  </div>
  <ul class="log news">
    <% news.each do |item| %>
      <li>
        <span class="d"><%= item[:date].iso8601 %></span>
        <% if item[:kind] == "post" %>
          <span><a href="<%= item[:url] %>"><%= item[:title] %></a><span class="tag">post</span></span>
        <% else %>
          <span>
            <a href="<%= item[:url] %>"><%= item[:version] %></a><span class="sum">: <%= item[:summary] %></span>
            <% if item[:new_cops].any? %>
              <br><span class="sum">new: <%== item[:new_cops].first(3).map { |name| cop_link(name) }.join(", ") %><%= ", ..." if item[:new_cops].size > 3 %></span>
            <% end %>
          </span>
        <% end %>
      </li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="s" data-sec>
  <div class="txt">
    <h2>History</h2>
    <p>I started RuboCop in April 2012 to make it easy to actually follow the <a href="<%= site.metadata.style_guide_url %>">Ruby Style Guide</a>, which I'd started a few months earlier. Fourteen years, two parsers and over 1,100 contributors later, it's how a big chunk of the Ruby world keeps its code in shape.</p>
    <p class="more"><a href="<%= site.metadata.docs_url %>about/history.html">The longer version &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
  <ul class="log">
    <% site.data.history.each do |event| %>
      <li>
        <% if event[:commit] %><a class="h" href="<%= event[:url] %>"><%= event[:commit] %></a><% else %><span class="h">-------</span><% end %>
        <span class="d"><%= event[:date].iso8601 %></span>
        <span><%= code_spans event[:text] %></span>
      </li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="s full" id="funding" data-sec>
  <div class="txt">
    <h2>Who's behind it</h2>
    <p>RuboCop is free and maintained by a small core team of volunteers, with help from thousands of contributors. If your company's code goes through RuboCop every day, consider giving something back. It's what keeps the lights on and the releases coming.</p>
    <ul class="people">
      <% site.data.team.each do |member| %>
        <li><img src="https://github.com/<%= member[:github] %>.png?size=60" alt="" loading="lazy" width="30" height="30"><a href="https://github.com/<%= member[:github] %>"><%= member[:name] %></a></li>
      <% end %>
    </ul>
    <div class="fund">
      <a href="https://opencollective.com/rubocop"><b>Open Collective</b><span>The project's own fund, for individuals and companies.</span></a>
      <a href="https://github.com/sponsors/bbatsov"><b>GitHub Sponsors</b><span>Sponsor the maintainers directly.</span></a>
      <a href="https://tidelift.com/subscription/pkg/rubygems-rubocop"><b>Tidelift</b><span>For companies that want it on an invoice.</span></a>
    </div>
    <% { "organization" => ["organizations backing RuboCop on Open Collective", 20], "individual" => ["and individuals", 30] }.each do |kind, (label, count)| %>
      <p class="backers-label"># <%= label %></p>
      <div class="backers <%= kind %>">
        <% count.times do |i| %>
          <a href="https://opencollective.com/rubocop/<%= kind %>/<%= i %>/website" rel="noopener"><img src="https://opencollective.com/rubocop/<%= kind %>/<%= i %>/avatar.svg" alt="" loading="lazy"></a>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>
